# -*- mode: python ; coding: utf-8 -*-
"""WARPRAND-APP-001 - the standalone Windows build of the warp randomizer.

**One file**, at the owner's direction: the release is a single executable the
user double-clicks, and the zip holds nothing else but a brief README. That is
the form the ticket allows ("single ``.exe``"), and it is why there is no
``COLLECT`` below - an onedir build cannot be reduced to a lone executable,
because the ``.exe`` needs its runtime directory beside it.

This departs from ``DragonByte Z.spec``, which is an onedir ``COLLECT`` build.
The trade is measured and stated rather than hidden:

* **startup costs about four seconds.** A one-file build unpacks its bundled
  runtime to a temporary directory on every launch. Measured on the author's
  machine: 4.44 s from ``dist/``, 3.93 s from the extracted release archive -
  against well under a second for the onedir build this replaced. The window
  appears and is responsive; it is simply slower to appear.
* a self-extracting executable draws an anti-virus heuristic more readily than a
  plain directory of DLLs, which is why the shipped README explains the
  "unknown publisher" prompt.

If startup or AV friction ever outweighs the single-file convenience, restore
the onedir shape by giving ``EXE`` ``exclude_binaries=True`` and adding back the
``COLLECT`` step; nothing else in this file depends on the mode, and the three
release scripts would need their artifact path changed from
``dist/<name>.exe`` back to ``dist/<name>/<name>.exe``.

The single data file is the ROM profile catalog. ``webfoot.cli.load_catalog``
prefers a checkout's ``config/roms.yaml`` and otherwise falls back to
``tools.webfoot.data/roms.yaml``, which is the branch a frozen build takes - so
that file must travel or every ROM selection is refused.

Windows resource metadata
-------------------------

The executable carries the product's own identity, so a user who looks at the
file's properties learns what it is rather than reading a blank dialog:

* ``version='Buu-Fury-Warp-Randomizer.version.txt'`` supplies ProductName,
  FileDescription, FileVersion/ProductVersion, InternalName, OriginalFilename,
  CompanyName and the licence. The version there is the same ``0.1.0`` the
  window and the manifest report, and the name is the same one
  ``warprand.TOOL_NAME`` carries.
* ``icon='scripts/randomizer.ico'`` supplies the application icon, rendered from
  the project's own ``public/favicon.svg``.

Neither file may name a ticket, a branch, a checkout, a user or a local path: the
executable is what a stranger receives.

Nothing from the commercial ROM is bundled, and nothing can be: the profile
catalog is a table of addresses and hashes, not game content.

Trimmed payload
---------------

PySide6's PyInstaller hook collects the whole Qt SDK, so a QtWidgets-only
application otherwise ships QtQml, QtQuick, QtPdf, QtVirtualKeyboard and every
Qt translation - libraries a single window with three text fields, a button and
a progress bar never loads. The excluded set is named in ``_UNUSED_QT_PREFIXES``
below.

The released artifact measures **35.2 MB** as a one-file executable - one
compressed archive of the runtime plus a README - which is the only size figure
this file states, because it is the one that was measured.

Two things are deliberately NOT dropped:

* ``opengl32sw.dll``, the software OpenGL fallback. It is the largest remaining
  binary and it is kept on purpose: it is what makes the window render on a
  machine with no usable GL driver, and the ticket puts reliability above size.
* ``Qt6Network`` and the TLS plugins, which Qt's platform layer may reach for
  through QDesktopServices ("Open Folder").

The exclusions are asserted by the packaged smoke run, which drives the real
frozen executable.
"""

#: Python modules nothing here imports.
_UNUSED_QT_MODULES = [
    'PySide6.QtQml',
    'PySide6.QtQuick',
    'PySide6.QtQuickWidgets',
    'PySide6.QtPdf',
    'PySide6.QtPdfWidgets',
    'PySide6.QtVirtualKeyboard',
]

#: Native libraries and data belonging to those modules, matched on their
#: bundle-relative path with forward slashes.
_UNUSED_QT_PREFIXES = (
    'PySide6/Qt6Qml',
    'PySide6/Qt6Quick',
    'PySide6/Qt6Pdf',
    'PySide6/Qt6VirtualKeyboard',
    'PySide6/translations/',
)


def _is_unused_qt(relative: str) -> bool:
    normalised = relative.replace('\\', '/')
    return normalised.startswith(_UNUSED_QT_PREFIXES)


a = Analysis(
    ['tools/webfoot/warprand/_pyinstaller_entry.py'],
    pathex=['.'],
    binaries=[],
    datas=[('tools/webfoot/data/roms.yaml', 'tools/webfoot/data')],
    # The profile catalog is reached through a *string*
    # `importlib.resources.files("tools.webfoot.data")`, which PyInstaller cannot
    # follow, and in one place through a function-local import it follows only
    # incidentally. Naming the package makes its inclusion a declaration rather
    # than a coincidence; an independent review flagged the earlier reliance.
    hiddenimports=['tools.webfoot.data'],
    hookspath=[],
    hooksconfig={},
    runtime_hooks=[],
    excludes=_UNUSED_QT_MODULES,
    noarchive=False,
    optimize=0,
)

a.binaries = [item for item in a.binaries if not _is_unused_qt(item[0])]
a.datas = [item for item in a.datas if not _is_unused_qt(item[0])]

pyz = PYZ(a.pure)

# ONE FILE, at the owner's direction: the release is a single executable the user
# double-clicks, and the archive holds nothing else but a README. That is the form
# the ticket allows ("single .exe"), and it is why there is no COLLECT here - an
# onedir build cannot be reduced to a lone executable, because the .exe needs its
# runtime directory beside it. The cost is stated in the module docstring above.
exe = EXE(
    pyz,
    a.scripts,
    a.binaries,
    a.datas,
    [],
    name='Buu-Fury-Warp-Randomizer',
    icon='scripts/randomizer.ico',
    version='Buu-Fury-Warp-Randomizer.version.txt',
    debug=False,
    bootloader_ignore_signals=False,
    strip=False,
    upx=True,
    upx_exclude=[],
    runtime_tmpdir=None,
    console=False,
    disable_windowed_traceback=False,
    argv_emulation=False,
    target_arch=None,
    codesign_identity=None,
    entitlements_file=None,
)
